<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Bayern Munich=E2=80=99s 2-1 defeat to Lill=
e was arguably the most eye-catching of Wednesday night=E2=80=99s Bigger Cu=
p results, but anyone who tuned into proceedings at Stade Pierre-Mauroy wil=
l have been unsurprised by the outcome. Not so much an elite European tie a=
s a game of Name That Bayern Reserve, the German champions=E2=80=99 startin=
g lineup resembled a pale facsimile of its usual self. Their current knack-=
list includes such high-profile names as =E2=80=93 deep breath =E2=80=93 Ha=
rry Kane, Michael Olise, Alphonso Davies, Manuel Neuer, Serge Gnabry, Luis =
D=C3=ADaz, Jamal Musiala and Ismael Saibari. And with D=C3=ADaz and Musiala=
 only fit enough for places on the bench, Konrad Laimer was deployed as a m=
akeshift striker. The veteran right-back is famously as handy as a small po=
t, but filling Kane=E2=80=99s iconic Skechers proved a task too far for the=
 Austrian.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Lille=E2=80=99s winner came from a similar=
ly unlikely source of goals for the French side, with Aleksandar Pavlovic p=
opping up to score a hilarious own goal that left his Bayern teammates givi=
ng him the collective stink-eye as he sank to his knees, head in hands in t=
he traditional <em>mea culpa</em> style. Having seen Ethan Mbapp=C3=A9 play=
ed clean through courtesy of a lightning-fast Lille counterattack, Pavlovic=
 sprinted back towards his own team=E2=80=99s goal like a cheating spouse w=
ho=E2=80=99s just realised they left their phone unlocked on the coffee tab=
le. Although his panicked chase looked to be in vain, the German hit pay-di=
rt when Mbapp=C3=A9 rolled his low shot past Bayern goalkeeper Jonas Urbig,=
 only to see it rebound off the foot of the post. Cue Pavlovic, who acciden=
tally stabbed the ball into the gaping goal as he attempted what appeared t=
o be the routine task of putting it out for a corner.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">While Pavlovic=E2=80=99s own goal was defi=
nitely the funniest Bayern have scored in recent memory, it certainly wasn=
=E2=80=99t the most embarrassing. That dubious honour goes to assorted club=
 blazers including Karl-Heinz Rummenigge, Uli Hoeness and Jan-Christian Dre=
esen, who have publicly questioned whether Musiala is worth the astronomica=
l wages he is paid, given his struggles with knack and recent diagnosis wit=
h a treatable neurological disorder. Rummenigge even went so far as to sugg=
est that if the 23-year-old does not return to top form, the remaining four=
 years of his contract would =E2=80=9Cno longer be justifiable=E2=80=9D and=
 suggested a pay cut might be in order. Asked about this tone-deaf and deci=
dedly mercenary invasion of his teammate=E2=80=99s medical privacy by club =
officials, Joshua Kimmich gave his superiors both barrels. =E2=80=9CWhat I =
don=E2=80=99t understand is the way this is being communicated in public,=
=E2=80=9D he harrumphed. =E2=80=9CIt must never happen that the burden is p=
laced on a player. The club is obviously the top priority and bigger than a=
 player, but it=E2=80=99s not bigger than a person. That=E2=80=99s an absol=
ute no-go and it is not Bayern-like.=E2=80=9D Neither is the shame of being=
 second in the Bundesliga while languishing in 15th place in Bigger Cup Mon=
ster Group, but don=E2=80=99t expect anyone in the club boardroom to volunt=
eer to trim their wages accordingly.</p>
